{:edb/db-before-t 3743 :edb/db-after-t 3744 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537056 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:36:09.310Z" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186055073 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186055073 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186055498 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-5$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186055498 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $-5$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186064503 1071 "![](images/f4/f49ed44236dcfabd3276187899bd2d7d300cbc1c280c228cda0bc2e3599fe7a8.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186064503 1071 "Which of the curves below are concave up?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186106216 1071 "![](images/10/102620aa55d15a8c07dc8224fd4de06d91cbd2abbbde6ee812df85a4862dc566.png)\n\nWhich of the above polygons are complex?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186106216 1071 "Which of the below polygons are complex?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186106216 1074 "A polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is called **complex**.\n\nAmong the given polygons, only polygon II has intersecting sides. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186106216 1074 "A polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is called **complex**.\n\nAmong the given polygons, only polygon II has intersecting sides. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is polygon II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186110929 1071 "![](images/bb/bb96a7692787ca6e055b41d4f1599b87baa1ea331544ea42235a7131343c76e3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186110929 1071 "Which of the diagrams below shows three collinear points?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186110929 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/54/54735541d4dfc424fe186ddabe0c3f9d3cbd45a0dc7a1b8f4551cf8c5e5bc14f.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/f0/f00f882c97fd201178e54f29151a48a4f41d4108cf375104cc338e968c55761b.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/6e/6e0c0320775d93364e32655d1e09081382078fa7e96fbe621a8c558f63dfcabb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186110929 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/54/54735541d4dfc424fe186ddabe0c3f9d3cbd45a0dc7a1b8f4551cf8c5e5bc14f.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/f0/f00f882c97fd201178e54f29151a48a4f41d4108cf375104cc338e968c55761b.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/6e/6e0c0320775d93364e32655d1e09081382078fa7e96fbe621a8c558f63dfcabb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186124945 1071 "![](images/8e/8e62c3485aed6c52001cc7ad8eda5c1eb09cd4873d6b740eb7595413dafa15e0.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186124945 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186125018 1071 "![](images/ca/caeed93f57e554d6bf79b8915935261abb39c6fe54cc6d7d349ff60502a96053.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186125018 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186172664 1071 "![](images/1b/1b0ced561cd98f9cf81c7de42bee7411a39595a9c59f2edaad0121d712dad157.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186172664 1071 "Which of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186172664 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186172664 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is curves I and IV only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186172669 1071 "![](images/00/0045eda99f40f774d2ef21214522eebb0c53bffee304ba503a9076a7491cc648.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186172669 1071 "Which of the regions shown below are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186172669 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains two holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186172669 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains two holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is regions I and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186193312 1071 "![](images/eb/ebeebae27d24ae4dd342b9cf65f93fe58e77aa8dcd99a7cde0880f68378862fa.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186193312 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186193319 1071 "![](images/df/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186193319 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186193319 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186193319 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186204256 1071 "![](images/fd/fdbcfe164fcca0e005255de10f10c10bedc156f3a582eeb5800322d4c4ebe0ab.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186204256 1071 "Which of the plots below represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186204256 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186204256 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is plot I only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186244143 1071 "![](images/89/89b0be0ca7ae1a6e76d32bfc669d6f554deaaad2055b2875e3f6a49821043205.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186244143 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186244144 1071 "![](images/a6/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186244144 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186247121 1071 "![](images/8c/8c695ea0cb09807c22fa655182945e838a7c5e454680176add27748945d143e5.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186247121 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186247121 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186247121 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186247148 1071 "![](images/f2/f2ed737550ddf5502d2a066cdc279285b624000a6c2ad8464496c122ad31a3ab.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186247148 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186247148 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But, \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186247148 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But, \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram I only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186247149 1071 "![](images/81/815f5ce1dd5bf3bc30b7853b53cd97736ffeb8224f30bc864cc7e43e07bedb5a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186247149 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186272306 1071 "![](images/9d/9d5ea7e13fa03ac553893aa78aa7ee32ac7b67f2eb19dff32228d53bf2010aa2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186272306 1071 "Which of the curves below are concave down?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186272307 1071 "![](images/b2/b2b904027ec6319fe5857f81089469568322d148e49cdf31bb20c07f1f31daf4.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186272307 1071 "Which of the curves below are concave down?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275420 1071 "![](images/73/7359571270dd1ce7df2b68d396e2c87cbf44bd7b18adf94bdb7ade4c02a8bc12.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275420 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275441 1071 "![](images/00/003b4f77a413444989bcec8b9682465419a58ded55ed95cefb4f43c122bdc3d3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275441 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275441 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275441 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275442 1071 "![](images/27/2739a0225640ff20cc9678edf8c41ba45ff637b17a40d0322ca792bef1346f06.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275442 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275442 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275442 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275443 1071 "![](images/42/42b23af4df7892df46461b01fd549ca56723dde0c08d3e88b71ea119bf45ba23.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275443 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186275443 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186275443 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186287684 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-4}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186287684 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $\\sqrt{-4}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186317172 1071 "![](images/d4/d4bd3b326c89316bc916658421bcd4c65c32a72672af9fbd7aa5397cb53ab374.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186317172 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186317172 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186317172 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186317349 1071 "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186317349 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186317349 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186317349 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186323703 1071 "![](images/ec/ec79ffa15b3ed25d8f439f575e1cdffc3122ae1d82e1b55500a6e5e2e17cf4fc.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186323703 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186323703 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace.  Every subspace must contain the origin,  but from the diagram, we can easily see that the origin is not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$  without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186323703 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace.  Every subspace must contain the origin,  but from the diagram, we can easily see that the origin is not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$  without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186323748 1071 "![](images/1d/1dd593e8b48b13ec512a3e0c823ec0aaac4f5341bd519c7bb9b04648f95f4251.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186323748 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186335886 1071 "![](images/38/3897b7fcf079b2522cbd0138631a894cbca1005cd241524cf9f3995965278612.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186335886 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186348364 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186348364 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186348365 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186348365 1071 "To which of the following sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186362290 1071 "![](images/d1/d1b6a9fa72404e765796d75fc7ba00a391317ef2670e2f65f7c43db32207e8d2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186362290 1071 "Which of the curves below is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186362290 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is not simple.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186362290 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is not simple.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is curve II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186362310 1071 "![](images/0d/0db1568fab302ddbd9f16dbdf9c276eeb62cbac37346b077c1639fe68c2396ac.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186362310 1071 "Which of the regions shown below are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186362310 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186362310 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is regions I, II and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186377893 1071 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186377893 1071 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186377893 1074 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186377893 1074 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186380386 1071 "![](images/05/05e7d61ebc2cc2b3d36915d448f6d9a6498376b858f72294f2ffc9342af38a7a.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186380386 1071 "Which of the curves below is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186380386 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is not simple.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186380386 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is not simple.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is curve III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186381826 1071 "$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}y=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186381826 1071 "$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,{x}y=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}\\in (1,2),\\,x{z}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186381826 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overbrace{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}^{\\text{scope of }∀z})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186381826 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $xy=1$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ immediately after $∀$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ in $xz>3$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overbrace{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}^{\\text{scope of }∀z})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $x$ in $xy=1$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186381867 1071 "$$\n∀{\\,y}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}\\le {\\,z}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186381867 1071 "$$\n∀{\\,y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\le {\\,z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186381867 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+x\\le z\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $z.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\le {z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrences are \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186381867 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $y$ immediately after $∀$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+x\\le z\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $y+x\\le z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ on the right side of $y+x\\le z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $z.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\le {z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrences are “The occurrence of $x$ in $y+x\\le z$”; “The occurrence of $z$ on the right side of $y+x\\le z$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186381874 1071 "$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>{n}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}-1={y}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186381874 1071 "$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186381874 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃z}\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186381874 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $n$ in $x>n+1$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ in ${z}^{3}-1=y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃z}\\,)\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $y$ on the right side of ${z}^{3}-1=y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $n$ in $x>n+1$”; “The occurrence of $y$ on the right side of ${z}^{3}-1=y$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186390205 1071 "$$\n∃{x}\\in R,\\,2-{x}={w}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186390205 1071 "$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186390205 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}^{\\text{scope of }∃x}\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186390205 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∃$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $2-x=w$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}^{\\text{scope of }∃x}\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186390212 1071 "$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}\\in Z,\\,{n}\\,|\\,{m}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186390212 1071 "$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186390212 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $m$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,n\\,|\\,m\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $m$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃m$ that uses $m:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,m\\in Z,\\,\\,\\overbrace{n\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃m}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186390212 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $m$ immediately after $∃$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,n\\,|\\,m\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $n$ on the left side of $n\\mid m$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- The occurrence of $m$ on the right side of $n\\mid m$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃m$ that uses $m:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,m\\in Z,\\,\\,\\overbrace{n\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃m}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $n$ on the left side of $n\\mid m$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186400944 1071 "![](images/7d/7d2f7497d6a11b4ceb994755498ef49bf0ce414601ab812dc55555c769a076ec.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186400944 1071 "Which of the curves below are concave up?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186400951 1071 "![](images/2f/2f07f2751398a101b41bc2c923fcdd7f6a5144da00008904297c36b2d8d44a30.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186400951 1071 "Which of the curves below are concave up?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186402516 1071 "![](images/91/91f073fa77aab423ee33902451e61ad9f033b61b2a875b66ff69708d4361e24f.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186402516 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186403237 1071 "![](images/23/239490fd3be930412d4f67ff89244607a5019d91f201bc4e579410b5610a18ed.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186403237 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186403244 1071 "![](images/be/be5b7625ad6f540f6769b121f52ccad69b669dbf764fc3bb68f89c99f066f145.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186403244 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186403251 1071 "![](images/f6/f65062d48189401ba22b1b08637d187eed532d5f93dca7663c2e80743510d5d7.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186403251 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407132 1071 "$$\n∀{k}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407132 1071 "$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407132 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $k$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,n+{k}^{2}\\ge k\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $k$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀k$ that uses $k:$\n\n$$\n∀k\\in (0,1),\\,\\overbrace{n+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∀k}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀\\,{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407132 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $k$ immediately after $∀$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,n+{k}^{2}\\ge k\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $n$ in $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- The occurrence of $k$ on the right side of $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀k$ that uses $k:$\n\n$$\n∀k\\in (0,1),\\,\\overbrace{n+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∀k}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀\\,{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $n$ in $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407134 1071 "$$\n(\\,∀{x}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407134 1071 "$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407134 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃y$ that uses $y:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃y})\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407134 1074 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∀$ in the left-hand statement is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,)\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $x=\\operatorname{sin} y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- The occurrence of $y$ in $\\operatorname{sin} y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃y$ that uses $y:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃y})\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $x$ in $x=\\operatorname{sin} y$” only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407336 1071 "![](images/42/42e0a9a9e987c1c56a335f29cf939e4f03d496fad148f148c32377893dbe7c13.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407336 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407336 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407336 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram I only." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186407337 1071 "![](images/1d/1d5d9586684c8606ca0f6df8dd70d9828ea04d3d9097b5a155ab66bcd7300ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186407337 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186416863 1071 "![](images/03/03ddf06402ad38708d232cf28783c817bf881965d54650eb158e7ffca4ea76a2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186416863 1071 "Which of the plots below represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186416863 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186416863 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is plots I and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417608 1071 "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417608 1071 "Which of the below digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417608 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417608 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is digraph I only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417609 1071 "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417609 1071 "Which of the plots below represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417609 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417609 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is plot II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417612 1071 "![](images/da/dac9030db59b2b51b2b95c8bc9b5988106be8d198b7f947d33c8678d1cfa3b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417612 1071 "Which of the below digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417612 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417612 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is digraph II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417615 1071 "![](images/70/70743ac83b876995835703b35fc133af2133960ea916f589d60c8ac5636ee4ef.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417615 1071 "Which of the below digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186417615 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186417615 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is digraphs I and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186418065 1071 "![](images/98/9836234d763ccb3da6b9ac0360dcb3662cacc5fc90d5ecc2dee20bf29ce0c77d.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186418065 1071 "Which of the curves below is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186418065 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is neither simple nor closed.\n\n- Curve II is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186418065 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is neither simple nor closed.\n\n- Curve II is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is curves III and IV only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186418246 1071 "![](images/1f/1fa323b54736f62bb334ae491d8d41deeba4a4e325f6e56f9651da147d50c349.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186418246 1071 "Which of the below digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186418246 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186418246 1074 "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is digraph II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186420406 1071 "![](images/9c/9cddd6584fee52757b1c7a470a6eeffee3678d569e36025ca70c99e5543c71c2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186420406 1071 "Which of the plots below represent a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186420406 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflective nor irreflexive, some of the points $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),$ $(\\beta ,\\beta ),$ and $(\\delta ,\\delta )$ must be in $R,$ and others must not.\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7d4257c9e249f9a4c2a53c5566292e21ae6dfa7f0e08e27042548da2d8e7e000.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to a relation that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive must contain some diagonal points, but not all of them. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186420406 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflective nor irreflexive, some of the points $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),$ $(\\beta ,\\beta ),$ and $(\\delta ,\\delta )$ must be in $R,$ and others must not.\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7d4257c9e249f9a4c2a53c5566292e21ae6dfa7f0e08e27042548da2d8e7e000.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to a relation that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive must contain some diagonal points, but not all of them. Therefore, the correct answer is plots I and II only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426531 1071 "![](images/8f/8f3c0e576491c4fd3897e30931ca7ed86d03c557ed37de86a3bcdc748f2263ea.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426531 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426531 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426531 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426532 1071 "![](images/5a/5ac6c0c6dc06d6e9b3970f2d6e5c5ce01a56c781143999d744a9b3914c37a49d.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426532 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426534 1071 "![](images/f6/f6695911ed29ccf88240660e45ce6527c0a10e3cf8d6a299ac9eef2be3ff53cb.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426534 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426534 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426534 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426536 1071 "![](images/c3/c36c34462f2ba7f129ba3370d4e67c5ac450b4188be629a256caf7807d4cb2d6.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426536 1071 "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186426536 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186426536 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186438788 1077 "![Graph I](images/74/749427225b10ed6998d384640273a2583f58ec596bb8ac9f006f53662e62850a.svg)" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186438788 1077 "I" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186438789 1077 "![Graph II](images/86/86abd8cebd946cacb0f6e6b5c36cf9292a3c5d99e471822307b0638377949b95.svg)" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186438789 1077 "II" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186438790 1077 "![Graph III](images/45/45c8195e54ab766f50439d63146e299f0602766b6c3d7c37942282db6e578185.svg)" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186438790 1077 "III" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186438791 1077 "![Graph IV](images/26/26893dd5faa2990bc8bfd53602863ee15fbad8008c52c59a3abd19b960904c70.svg)" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186438791 1077 "IV" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186438792 1077 "![Graph I](images/9b/9beab54a4175ffdc30be00f3465c1404ebaf5efdbad8b89dd3ab5e30294a1cba.svg)" 13194139537056 17592186576411 true] [17592186438792 1077 "I" 13194139537056 17592186576411 false] [17592186438793 1077 "![Graph II](images/e8/e8e1bf751c13bf478eb141af2ce76c1